====================================================================================================
V6 vs BOWLES, counted from the implementations
====================================================================================================
  Both transpiled to ['rz', 'sx', 'x', 'cx']. Circuit counts read from Bowles'
  own get_nefv_cost(), not from the paper's abstract.

  N=4, M=24, G=3, Bowles blocks B=24
                              method  circuits  depth/c   2q/c  2q total
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------
                                  V6         3       91    116       348
              Bowles, as implemented        24      449    158      3792
            Bowles, theoretical 2B-1        47      449    158      7426
                     parameter-shift       144       20      6       864

    V6 circuits vs Bowles-as-implemented : 8.0x fewer
    V6 2q total vs Bowles-as-implemented : 10.9x fewer
  ......................................................................
  N=6, M=36, G=3, Bowles blocks B=36
                              method  circuits  depth/c   2q/c  2q total
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------
                                  V6         3      102    134       402
              Bowles, as implemented        36      709    254      9144
            Bowles, theoretical 2B-1        71      709    254     18034
                     parameter-shift       216       22     10      2160

    V6 circuits vs Bowles-as-implemented : 12.0x fewer
    V6 2q total vs Bowles-as-implemented : 22.7x fewer
  ......................................................................

  'Bowles, theoretical 2B-1' is the figure the paper quotes and the notes
  repeated. It requires no CNOTs in the future block, which EfficientSU2
  violates by construction, so the row above it is the applicable one.

  The LCU circuits carry two CONTROLLED copies of the future ansatz, which
  is why their per-circuit cost is not comparable to a bare ansatz run.
